WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.037
Idempotency preserves one logical intent across repeated delivery.

00:00:04.187 --> 00:00:08.190
A client-generated key must remain stable for the same intent, and the

00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:10.860
server must bind it to relevant request fields.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.495
The same key with changed data should conflict.

00:00:13.645 --> 00:00:17.586
Persist the key and business result with a unique constraint so multiple

00:00:17.586 --> 00:00:19.228
instances share the same authority.

00:00:19.378 --> 00:00:22.568
A check-then-insert without a database constraint can race.

00:00:22.718 --> 00:00:27.060
Handle the losing concurrent insert outside its failed transaction, then read and

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:28.507
compare the winning record.

00:00:28.657 --> 00:00:32.778
Define retention: deleting old keys can allow an old retry to create

00:00:32.778 --> 00:00:33.808
a new effect.

00:00:33.958 --> 00:00:36.805
Scope keys by account in a multi-tenant system.

00:00:36.955 --> 00:00:39.768
Orders stores request_key with a unique constraint.

00:00:39.918 --> 00:00:44.304
A matching retry returns the same order; a changed quantity conflicts.

00:00:44.454 --> 00:00:49.287
The downstream reservation uses the stable order ID, so retries after an

00:00:49.287 --> 00:00:52.106
unknown inventory outcome cannot consume stock twice.

00:00:52.256 --> 00:00:57.481
The local demo has no authenticated accounts; tenant-scoped keys are an explicit

00:00:57.481 --> 00:00:58.352
production extension.

00:00:58.502 --> 00:01:03.371
Launch twenty concurrent requests using one checkout key, then repeat with different

00:01:03.371 --> 00:01:03.777
quantities.

00:01:03.927 --> 00:01:08.762
Verify one order, one reservation, and a conflict for mismatched intent.

00:01:08.912 --> 00:01:10.847
Design a key-retention policy.

00:01:10.997 --> 00:01:15.141
Count durable business effects rather than HTTP responses alone.

00:01:15.291 --> 00:01:19.826
A policy should cover the maximum expected retry window and any legal

00:01:19.826 --> 00:01:21.337
or business retention requirements.

00:01:21.487 --> 00:01:26.299
Hash a canonical request representation if the request grows beyond a few

00:01:26.299 --> 00:01:28.705
fields, and version the canonicalization rule.

00:01:28.855 --> 00:01:29.526
Question.

00:01:29.676 --> 00:01:32.623
Is a random key generated on every retry useful?

00:01:32.773 --> 00:01:33.349
Answer.

00:01:33.499 --> 00:01:33.983
No.

00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:38.275
It turns each retry into a new logical intent and defeats deduplication.

00:01:38.425 --> 00:01:39.096
Question.

00:01:39.246 --> 00:01:42.996
Can an in-memory map enforce idempotency across replicas?

00:01:43.146 --> 00:01:43.723
Answer.

00:01:43.873 --> 00:01:44.971
Not by itself.

00:01:45.121 --> 00:01:50.012
Replicas need shared durable coordination or a routing and recovery design that

00:01:50.012 --> 00:01:51.235
provides equivalent guarantees.
